\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Coordinate translation and phase multiplication}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{On a site #{v} of the coupled chain of \ref{fdip-00GV}, take
#{r=\eta e_v}, #{z=\ell e_v}. The
[Weyl operator](fdip-00H8) acts by
##{(W(\eta e_v,\ell e_v)f)(q)
 =e^{i\eta(q_v+\ell /2)}f(q+\ell e_v).}
It shifts one coordinate and multiplies by a phase. Translation
invariance of #{dq} and modulus one of the phase give
#{\|W(r,z)f\|=\|f\|}; the inverse is #{W(-r,-z)}.}

\p{The multiplication operator #{\Phi_v} is self-adjoint on
its stated maximal domain. The unitary Fourier transform sends
#{\Pi_v} to real multiplication by #{\xi_v}, proving the same
assertion for momentum. Cutoff followed by mollification
approximates both position and its product in #{L^2}; for momentum
it approximates #{f,\partial_vf} in #{L^2}. Thus #{C_c^\infty}
is a core for each operator. Fourier inversion then gives
#{(e^{iz\cdot\Pi}f)(q)=f(q+z)}, and hence
##{W(r,z)=e^{ir\cdot\Phi /2}e^{iz\cdot\Pi}e^{ir\cdot\Phi /2}.}
The dot products here are self-adjoint real multipliers after
position or Fourier transformation. These calculations specialize
\citet{Section 8.1, pp. 207-208}{teschl2014quantum} to the chosen coordinates.}

\p{For arbitrary real vectors, applying the two actions successively
gives the phase
#{r\cdot(q+z/2)+r'\cdot(q+z+z'/2)}. Subtracting the phase
#{(r+r')\cdot(q+(z+z')/2)} leaves
#{-\sigma((r,z),(r',z'))/2}. Therefore
##{W(r,z)W(r',z')
 =e^{-i\sigma((r,z),(r',z'))/2}W(r+r',z+z').}
For phase then translation at one site, the coefficient is
#{e^{-i\eta\ell /2}}; reversing the order changes its sign in
the exponent.}

\p{The [vacuum and thermal states](fdip-00H8) are normalized
and positive: the first is a unit vector state, and the second
uses the positive trace-one operator #{e^{-\beta H}/Z_\beta}.
For either state and bounded insertions,
##{|\omega(A_0\alpha_{t_1}(A_1)\cdots\alpha_{t_n}(A_n))|
 \le\prod_{j=0}^n\|A_j\|.}
Indeed each conjugation preserves operator norm and a positive
normalized state is bounded by that norm; for the thermal state
this also follows directly from the trace norm inequality.
The ground vector evolves by the phase #{e^{-itE_0}}, and
#{e^{-\beta H}} commutes with #{V_t}. These identities prove
stationarity of both states. In particular every Weyl correlation
above exists for arbitrary real times and has absolute value
at most one.}
